WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.013 --> 00:00:03.397
- [Announcer] In a city
that loves its bikes,

00:00:03.397 --> 00:00:06.512
there is one man looking out for the rest.

00:00:06.512 --> 00:00:10.747
One man, brave enough to
bring bike thieves to justice.

00:00:10.747 --> 00:00:13.664
A man, the city calls, bike Batman.

00:00:14.865 --> 00:00:17.782
- In reality, I'm just an engineer.

00:00:24.919 --> 00:00:28.018
Back in the early 2000's,
I started really noticing,

00:00:28.018 --> 00:00:29.882
you know, there was a lot of bike theft.

00:00:29.882 --> 00:00:32.237
I remember I had a set of
wheels stolen, and I was livid,

00:00:32.237 --> 00:00:35.370
but I was super poor, there
was nothing to do, you know,

00:00:35.370 --> 00:00:36.848
I couldn't recover them.

00:00:36.848 --> 00:00:40.105
It was so frustrating for me,
knowing that the police are

00:00:40.105 --> 00:00:43.107
so busy with so many other
crimes in Seattle, that there

00:00:43.107 --> 00:00:47.546
was nobody following up
on these minor incidents.

00:00:47.546 --> 00:00:50.240
- [Announcer] Enough was
enough, he realized he needed to

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:52.709
take the law into his owns hands,

00:00:52.709 --> 00:00:55.299
and he had the perfect plan.

00:00:55.299 --> 00:00:59.693
- I would best describe bike
index as a data base, um,

00:00:59.693 --> 00:01:02.652
that allows you to register your bicycle.

00:01:02.652 --> 00:01:05.199
It's a great way to
search for stolen bikes.

00:01:05.199 --> 00:01:08.617
The way that it works for me,
is as I'm perusing craigslist,

00:01:08.617 --> 00:01:11.643
I'll notice a fishy ad, and
I will immediately open up

00:01:11.643 --> 00:01:16.245
bike index, and do a quick
search for any bikes that

00:01:16.245 --> 00:01:19.757
have been stolen in that geographic area.

00:01:19.757 --> 00:01:21.274
The first time that I recovered a bike,

00:01:21.274 --> 00:01:22.723
I had no idea what I was doing.

00:01:22.723 --> 00:01:26.113
I drove after work to go meet
this guy, and it wasn't one

00:01:26.113 --> 00:01:29.916
person I was meeting, it
was three, and I told them,

00:01:29.916 --> 00:01:32.667
you know guys, the cops are
on their way I just called ‘em.

00:01:32.667 --> 00:01:34.407
This bike is stolen,
and you're not gonna be

00:01:34.407 --> 00:01:35.727
leaving with it.

00:01:35.727 --> 00:01:37.272
Eventually I threw the bike in my truck,

00:01:37.272 --> 00:01:40.543
burned out, returned
the bike, it felt great.

00:01:40.543 --> 00:01:42.741
- [Announcer] What began
as a simple plan to help

00:01:42.741 --> 00:01:46.827
a fellow cyclist, took
on a life of it's own.

00:01:46.827 --> 00:01:50.077
Seattle's own bike vigilante, was born.

00:01:50.997 --> 00:01:55.107
- We were able to recover,
high 30's or low 40's of bikes.

00:01:55.107 --> 00:01:58.408
I'm not entirely sure
when the bike vigilante

00:01:58.408 --> 00:01:59.795
had become a thing.

00:01:59.795 --> 00:02:02.851
It was probably when the
bike Batman moniker was given

00:02:02.851 --> 00:02:06.382
to me, and this is just
something, you know, I like doing.

00:02:06.382 --> 00:02:09.022
I don't need, or want,
you know, any recognition.

00:02:09.022 --> 00:02:12.600
Just doing good is essentially, you know,
how I try to live my life.

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:14.866
- [Announcer] And thanks to bike Batman,

00:02:14.866 --> 00:02:19.245
the cyclists of the Emerald
City, can rest easy,

00:02:19.245 --> 00:02:20.078
once more.

